The keystones are the twenty cut gems set out by the host of the [[Phenomena/Pilgrimage|pilgrimage]] at [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]] as the price of a place aboard his strider. The one he showed from the scaffolding is a faceted cerulean stone floating in an embossed golden box; the other nineteen were placed in or around [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]]. A keystone buys passage for its bearer and one vehicle with whatever it will carry, and two keystones buy two vehicles. The only rule is that the bearer hold a keystone in his hands when the strider leaves, six months after the night of the announcement; the host cares nothing for how it was come by or who found it. Guests learn more about the stones by trading at the party or by taking on the games along the street.

Once aboard, a keystone is planted in a ritual circle on its bearer's plot of land and grown into a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]]. The stone opens a way into a pocket of the void: a stone dais floating in empty space, ringed by turning arms of stone carved with magic, with bowl-shaped platforms floating off it, each filled with pale blue sand. The place puts a viewer in mind of the starscape seen where the [[Divinity/Outer Gods/Watcher With 1,000 Eyes|Watcher]]'s presence is strong, though it is not quite the same. Small creatures, the [[Bestiary/Thoughts|thoughts]], rise out of the sand, and killing them feeds their energy to the keystone until the heart within it is ripe.

Keystone 14 belonged to a Heart calling itself [[Org/Prime Meridian/Rapid Connection|Rapid Connection]], a collector of paintings in oil or acrylic on canvas. The Party took it, having traded the Heart the [[People/Kellum Quinn|Kellum Quinn]] *Second Meaning* for the stone and for the waiver of all Prime Meridian fees. Every rival who hunted it is dead: [[People/Sbrtlby Jotes|Sbrtlby Jotes]] and his hired henchmen, and [[Org/Concordia/People/Crawford Reeves|Crawford Reeves]] and [[Org/Concordia/People/Louis Maller|Louis Maller]] of [[Org/Concordia/index|Concordia]]. The Party planted keystone 14 at berth 14 on the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]] and grew from it the heart [[Org/Iconoclasm/Eerie|Eerie]].

The holder of keystone 15 would trade it for a relic of the [[Org/Iridescent Church/index|Iridescent Church]], a reliquary or a holy book. Second-Among-Equals [[People/Thistle Monroe|Thistle Monroe]] is the one most likely to knowingly lead to it.

Before the departure the host spoke through the Party's keystone. He said the nineteen stones placed around the city were locked away in one place or another, and that the strider would leave at 9am from the same place the gathering first set out from. The Party is aboard, believing the pilgrimage is bound for wherever the singer of the [[Phenomena/Harmony/Voidsong|Voidsong]] is.

Once the strider is under way the stone glows as the guests' tithe is drawn, and shows a number that falls as the [[Org/The Harlequins/People/The Master of Ceremonies|Master of Ceremonies]] takes his share of their energy. At the banquet the evening the [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]] left [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]] a keystone read 100 and fell to 90 over about half a minute, then to 80 during the tithe.

A keystone grown into a [[Phenomena/Hearts|Heart]] serves its holder as a totem, and after the first tithe its face shows three readings: the time left before the next tithe, the cost of that tithe, and the energy the holder's heart holds. Early in the crossing those read a little under nine days, a cost of 21, and an energy the Party raised from 80 to 90 by feeding their heart the core of a dead Heart. At the second tithe, on the night [[Org/Verdant Holdings/index|Verdant Holdings]] was destroyed and [[Org/NecroLog/index|NecroLog]]'s holding went fallow, the stone read a cost of 29 and, once the tithe had been drawn, 34. The Party had taken the total drawn from all the holds to be a constant 400, and read the jump in what their own Heart owed as the share of two holds that were no longer paying.
